  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe synoptic scale trough over the eastern CONUS, anchored by the\nongoing Nor'easter over New England coastline today, will begin \nshifting east today, allowing upper ridging to begin building in \nfrom our west tonight through late this week. Subsequently\ntemperatures will respond, warming significantly over the next\ncouple of days. Highs will climb into the low 80s in the north by\nWednesday, and into the upper 80s to perhaps low 90s for the\ncentral and southern low elevation areas. It won't be record\nterritory, but quite warm nonetheless. Otherwise, dry conditions \ncontinue through Thursday before rain chances make a return \nsometime Friday into the weekend.\n\nSpeaking of the rain, there's rather good agreement amongst \nguidance on how the overall pattern evolves late this week into \nthe weekend. An upper low will move ENE from the plains towards \nthe Ohio valley late Thursday, opening up as it does so in \nresponse to a strong shortwave moving through the Canadian side of\nthe Great Lakes region. Surface high pressure then builds across \nthe Midwest and Ohio valley areas, leading to a cold front being \npositioned somewhere across from the Ohio valley southwest into \nWest Tennessee by Friday, pushing south of the region by Saturday.\nMeanwhile upper high pressure will remain anchored off the \nFlorida and southeast coastline, with broad southwesterly flow \npumping Gulf moisture northward into the Tennessee valley and \nsouthern Appalachian region Friday into the weekend. The only \nuncertainty to speak of with how this pattern plays out, revolves\naround when exactly the rain makes a return. Current NBM guidance\nhas PoPs in the forecast for Friday afternoon which is not \nunreasonable. But there still is a decent signal that suggests \nrain holds off until Saturday. For now, will continue to side with\nNBM guidance until there is clarity in the timing. Regardless of\nthe onset, the pattern suggests the rainy conditions continue\nthrough the weekend before another trough and associated cold\nfront dive southeast from Minnesota and kick the rain east of us\nsometime Sunday.",
